course: dictionary
lesson: 5745
title: One word, more than one meaning
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and lounge is one of them. To find the right meaning, look at the words around it. Each sentence gives you clues.
explain: «Freya's mum comes into the lounge just as an 18 film appears on the screen.» A screen and a room where people sit to watch it tell you that a lounge is a room in a house. Here it is a noun, a place.
explain: Look at the word form too. In «a lounge» it is a thing you can be in. In «to lounge» it is an action: «Aroha lounged on the sofa» would mean sitting or lying back in a relaxed way. Swap your meaning into the sentence to check it.
fact: A lounge is a comfortable room in a house where people sit and relax. In Britain, Australia and New Zealand, it is often the main sitting room.
fact: Many Americans say "living room" for the same room.
fact: A lounge can also be a waiting room, such as a departure lounge at an airport.
fact: To lounge means to sit or lie in a lazy, relaxed way, as in "lounge around".
fact: Some schools have a staff lounge, a room where teachers can rest.
exercise: «Freya's mum comes into the lounge just as an 18 film appears on the screen.» What is the lounge here? | A bathroom with a bath | *A room in a house where people sit and relax | A kitchen where food is cooked | A shop at the airport
exercise: Which phrase uses lounge to mean a waiting room? | Lounge around at home | A lounge chair | *A departure lounge at the airport | A lounge lampshade
exercise: «Aroha cracked a lampshade trampolining in the lounge.» What does lounge mean here? | A type of trampoline | A waiting room at an airport | To sit lazily | *A living room in a house
test: Which sentence uses lounge as a verb? | The lounge is at the top of the stairs. | *Dad likes to lounge on the sofa after work. | The departure lounge was very busy. | The lounge had a big sofa and a rug.
test: In Australia and New Zealand, families often call the main sitting room what? | The porch | The garden shed | *The lounge | The attic
exam: Which check helps you decide what lounge means in a sentence? | Count the letters in the word | *Swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense | Skip the sentence altogether | Look only at the first letter
